        <div class=3D"article-content">

         =20
          <p>Let=E2=80=99s say you created a lovely vector illustration in =
Adobe Illustrator. Or you=E2=80=99ve used Illustrator to finesse some exist=
ing graphics. Or for literally any reason at all, you have a file open in A=
dobe Illustrator that you ultimately want to use on the web as SVG. </p>
<p>There are several different ways of getting SVG out of Illustrator, each=
 one a bit different. Let=E2=80=99s take a look.</p>
<p>TL;DR: Exporting, like <strong>File &gt; Export &gt; Export As=E2=80=A6<=
/strong> SVG then optimizing is your best bet for the web.</p>
<p><span id=3D"more-247358"></span></p>
<h3><a href=3D"https://css-tricks.com/illustrator-to-svg/#aa-the-save-as-me=
thod" aria-hidden=3D"true" class=3D"aal_anchor" id=3D"aa-the-save-as-method=
"><svg aria-hidden=3D"true" class=3D"aal_svg" height=3D"16" version=3D"1.1"=
 viewBox=3D"0 0 16 16" width=3D"16"><path fill-rule=3D"evenodd" d=3D"M4 9h1=
v1H4c-1.5 0-3-1.69-3-3.5S2.55 3 4 3h4c1.45 0 3 1.69 3 3.5 0 1.41-.91 2.72-2=
 3.25V8.59c.58-.45 1-1.27 1-2.09C10 5.22 8.98 4 8 4H4c-.98 0-2 1.22-2 2.5S3=
 9 4 9zm9-3h-1v1h1c1 0 2 1.22 2 2.5S13.98 12 13 12H9c-.98 0-2-1.22-2-2.5 0-=
.83.42-1.64 1-2.09V6.25c-1.09.53-2 1.84-2 3.25C6 11.31 7.55 13 9 13h4c1.45 =
0 3-1.69 3-3.5S14.5 6 13 6z"></path></svg></a>The =E2=80=9CSave As=E2=80=A6=
=E2=80=9D Method</h3>
<p>Illustrator supports SVG as a first-class file format. You can <strong>F=
ile &gt; Save As=E2=80=A6</strong> and choose =E2=80=9CSVG=E2=80=9D as an o=
ption, as an alternative to the default `.ai` file format. </p>
<figure id=3D"post-247359" class=3D"align-none media-247359"><img data-reca=
lc-dims=3D"1" decoding=3D"async" src=3D"https://i0.wp.com/css-tricks.com/wp=
-content/uploads/2016/11/svg-import-saveas-.png?ssl=3D1" alt=3D""><figcapti=
on>Using Edit &gt; Save As=E2=80=A6 in Illustrator</figcaption></figure>
<p>There is even a button in the Save SVG options that come up called =E2=
=80=9CSVG Code=E2=80=A6=E2=80=9D you can click to have Illustrator show you=
 the code before saving it, presumably for copy-and-paste purposes.</p>
<figure id=3D"post-247360" class=3D"align-none media-247360"><img data-reca=
lc-dims=3D"1" decoding=3D"async" src=3D"https://i0.wp.com/css-tricks.com/wp=
-content/uploads/2016/11/svg-import-saveas-copy.gif?ssl=3D1" alt=3D""></fig=
ure>
<p>If you search the web a bit about the differences between saving in diff=
erent formats from Illustrator, you=E2=80=99ll find plenty of generic infor=
mation telling you that SVG is for use on THE WORLD WIDE WEB, so if that=E2=
=80=99s where you intend to use this graphic, that=E2=80=99s the format you=
 should save in. </p>
<p><strong>Be careful here.</strong> Saving as SVG from Illustrator is fine=
, but the file is absolutely not ready for direct use on the web. When you =
=E2=80=9CSave As=E2=80=A6=E2=80=9D from Illustrator as SVG, Illustrators pr=
imary concern is that you can open that file again in Illustrator just as y=
ou left it. </p>
<p>For example, Illustrator has proprietary features that aren=E2=80=99t a =
part of SVG. A simple example: guides. You won=E2=80=99t lose your guides s=
aving as SVG this way, they are preserved just fine. But guides are meaning=
less in SVG on the web, so you=E2=80=99d be sending useless data if you use=
 SVG saved this way directly on the web.</p>
<p>The file size of =E2=80=9CSave As=E2=80=A6=E2=80=9D saved Illustrator SV=
G=E2=80=99s can be several orders of magnitude larger than a really web-rea=
dy version. Take a look at the CSS-Tricks logo =E2=80=9CSave As=E2=80=A6=E2=
=80=9D=E2=80=98d and one exported for the web.</p>
<figure id=3D"post-247991" class=3D"align-none media-247991"><img data-reca=
lc-dims=3D"1" decoding=3D"async" src=3D"https://i0.wp.com/css-tricks.com/wp=
-content/uploads/2016/11/file-sizes.png?ssl=3D1" alt=3D""><figcaption>410 K=
B for the =E2=80=9CSave As=E2=80=A6=E2=80=9D version vs. 3 KB for the expor=
ted version.</figcaption></figure>
<p>It=E2=80=99s not entirely clear to us why =E2=80=9CSave As=E2=80=A6=E2=
=80=9Ding as SVG would be a good idea. You may just want to keep files as `=
.ai` until you want to actually export for the web. Or if you do save as SV=
G, you might as well use the =E2=80=9CPreserve Illustrator Editing Capabili=
ties=E2=80=9D checkbox and use a naming convention that is clear this is an=
 =E2=80=9Coriginal=E2=80=9D file, not a web-ready one.</p>
<p>Wait, =E2=80=9Cexported version=E2=80=9D? Let=E2=80=99s look at that nex=
t.</p>
<h3><a href=3D"https://css-tricks.com/illustrator-to-svg/#aa-the-export-as-=
method" aria-hidden=3D"true" class=3D"aal_anchor" id=3D"aa-the-export-as-me=
thod"><svg aria-hidden=3D"true" class=3D"aal_svg" height=3D"16" version=3D"=
1.1" viewBox=3D"0 0 16 16" width=3D"16"><path fill-rule=3D"evenodd" d=3D"M4=
 9h1v1H4c-1.5 0-3-1.69-3-3.5S2.55 3 4 3h4c1.45 0 3 1.69 3 3.5 0 1.41-.91 2.=
72-2 3.25V8.59c.58-.45 1-1.27 1-2.09C10 5.22 8.98 4 8 4H4c-.98 0-2 1.22-2 2=
.5S3 9 4 9zm9-3h-1v1h1c1 0 2 1.22 2 2.5S13.98 12 13 12H9c-.98 0-2-1.22-2-2.=
5 0-.83.42-1.64 1-2.09V6.25c-1.09.53-2 1.84-2 3.25C6 11.31 7.55 13 9 13h4c1=
.45 0 3-1.69 3-3.5S14.5 6 13 6z"></path></svg></a>The =E2=80=9CExport As=E2=
=80=9D Method</h3>
<p><strong>File &gt; Export &gt; Export As=E2=80=A6</strong> is very differ=
ent. You are =E2=80=9Cexporting=E2=80=9D, and the implication is that the f=
ile that is generated is no longer an Illustrator-friendly and editable fil=
e, but a new file specifically for some other purpose. </p>
<p>JPG is one of the options, for example. You wouldn=E2=80=99t expect to b=
e able to edit your vector art after exporting it as a JPG.</p>
<p>SVG is an option here too. The output is vastly different than =E2=80=9C=
Save As=E2=80=A6=E2=80=9D. Exported SVGs actually are pretty close to web-r=
eady. There is no weird doctype, loads of metadata, or proprietary Illustra=
tor stuff. Exported SVG likely will not open in Illustrator in exactly the =
same way it was in the original file. </p>
<p>You=E2=80=99ll get a minimal export options screen, like this:</p>
<figure id=3D"post-247993" class=3D"align-none media-247993"><img data-reca=
lc-dims=3D"1" decoding=3D"async" src=3D"https://i0.wp.com/css-tricks.com/wp=
-content/uploads/2016/11/export-svg-options.png?ssl=3D1" alt=3D""><figcapti=
on>The SVG Options when exporting SVG.</figcaption></figure>

<p>The options shown there are good defaults. Quick overview:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Styling:</strong> =E2=80=9CPresentation Attributes=E2=80=9D mea=
ns stuff like fill: red; rather than =E2=80=9CInline Styles=E2=80=9D which =
means style=3D=E2=80=9Dfill: red;=E2=80=9D. Presentation attributes are eas=
ier to override in CSS. Inline styles provide more style resilience. There =
is also an option to export styles in a <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> block wi=
thin the SVG, which <em>may</em> be efficient on SVG with lots of similar e=
lements.</li>
<li><strong>Font:</strong> =E2=80=9CSVG=E2=80=9D means to use <text> elemen=
ts (and friends), which is extremely efficient, provided the fonts you=E2=
=80=99ve used are available on the web site you intended to use it on. =E2=
=80=9CConvert to Outlines=E2=80=9D will turn the text into vector shapes on=
 export, making sure it will look exactly right, but losing efficient, acce=
ssibility, searchability, and copy-ability.</text></li>
<li><strong>Images:</strong> =E2=80=9CLink=E2=80=9D means that if there hap=
pens to be raster graphics within the SVG, it will link out to them rather =
than embedding them within the SVG, versus =E2=80=9CEmbed=E2=80=9Dding them=
.</li>
<li><strong>Object ID=E2=80=99s:</strong> Unique ensures that every ID is u=
nique (good for the web), but you also have the option to make them very sh=
ort (=E2=80=9CMinimal=E2=80=9D) or be based on layer names.</li>
<li><strong>Decimal:</strong> 2 is probably fine. You=E2=80=99d only go up =
if you knew you were working with a really tiny viewBox and needed a lot of=
 precision, or down if you were working on a giant viewBox.</li>
<li><strong>Minify:</strong> We=E2=80=99re exporting for the web, so, yes.<=
/li>
<li><strong>Responsive:</strong> On means =E2=80=9Cdon=E2=80=99t put <code>=
width</code> and <code>height</code> attribute=E2=80=9D. Off means do. It a=
ctually might be smart leaving this off (<a href=3D"https://twitter.com/chr=
iscoyier/status/799294264446287872" rel=3D"noopener">see here</a>).</li>
</ul>
<p>Exporting this way is definitely good for the web. Although SVGO can sti=
ll squeak a little bit more efficiency out of it yet, depending on the grap=
hic of course.</p>
<figure id=3D"post-248000" class=3D"align-none media-248000"><img data-reca=
lc-dims=3D"1" decoding=3D"async" src=3D"https://i0.wp.com/css-tricks.com/wp=
-content/uploads/2016/11/svgo.png?ssl=3D1" alt=3D""></figure>
<h3><a href=3D"https://css-tricks.com/illustrator-to-svg/#aa-the-export-for=
-screens-method" aria-hidden=3D"true" class=3D"aal_anchor" id=3D"aa-the-exp=
ort-for-screens-method"><svg aria-hidden=3D"true" class=3D"aal_svg" height=
=3D"16" version=3D"1.1" viewBox=3D"0 0 16 16" width=3D"16"><path fill-rule=
=3D"evenodd" d=3D"M4 9h1v1H4c-1.5 0-3-1.69-3-3.5S2.55 3 4 3h4c1.45 0 3 1.69=
 3 3.5 0 1.41-.91 2.72-2 3.25V8.59c.58-.45 1-1.27 1-2.09C10 5.22 8.98 4 8 4=
H4c-.98 0-2 1.22-2 2.5S3 9 4 9zm9-3h-1v1h1c1 0 2 1.22 2 2.5S13.98 12 13 12H=
9c-.98 0-2-1.22-2-2.5 0-.83.42-1.64 1-2.09V6.25c-1.09.53-2 1.84-2 3.25C6 11=
.31 7.55 13 9 13h4c1.45 0 3-1.69 3-3.5S14.5 6 13 6z"></path></svg></a>The =
=E2=80=9CExport for Screens=E2=80=9D Method</h3>
<p>Since version CC 2017, Illustrator has an <strong>File &gt; Export &gt; =
Export for Screens=E2=80=A6</strong> method built specifically for exportin=
g for digital devices. I=E2=80=99d say =E2=80=9Cthe web=E2=80=9D, but it ha=
s features for exporting for iOS and Android, so =E2=80=9CScreens=E2=80=9D,=
 as they say, is appropriate. </p>
<figure id=3D"post-247967" class=3D"align-none media-247967"><img data-reca=
lc-dims=3D"1" decoding=3D"async" src=3D"https://i0.wp.com/css-tricks.com/wp=
-content/uploads/2016/11/svg-import-exportscreens.jpg?ssl=3D1" alt=3D""></f=
igure>
<p>This brings up an options dialog allowing us to select our artwork in di=
fferent ways and exports the parts we want in the formats we want.</p>
<figure id=3D"post-247968" class=3D"align-none media-247968"><img data-reca=
lc-dims=3D"1" decoding=3D"async" src=3D"https://i0.wp.com/css-tricks.com/wp=
-content/uploads/2016/11/svg-import-exportscreens-2.jpg?ssl=3D1" alt=3D""><=
figcaption>The =E2=80=9CExport for Screens=E2=80=9D options screen</figcapt=
ion></figure>
<p>This is really convenient UI! We can export just parts of our illustrati=
on in not only SVG, but other formats as well, at the same exact time. Pict=
ure an icon system. 20 artboards in a single document, and with one command=
 you export all of them as SVG and multiple resolutions of PNG. Pretty nice=
.</p>
<p>Under the hood, it appears to be using the same system as =E2=80=9CExpor=
t=E2=80=9D, so you=E2=80=99ll get web-optimized output.</p>
<p>Artboards is one way of splitting up artwork to be exported separately. =
 There is also an Asset Export panel that allows you to drag-and-drop bits =
of artwork intended to be output separately. </p>
<figure id=3D"post-248002" class=3D"align-none media-248002"><img data-reca=
lc-dims=3D"1" decoding=3D"async" src=3D"https://i0.wp.com/css-tricks.com/wp=
-content/uploads/2016/11/export.gif?ssl=3D1" alt=3D""></figure>
<h3><a href=3D"https://css-tricks.com/illustrator-to-svg/#aa-the-copy-direc=
tly-from-the-artboard-method" aria-hidden=3D"true" class=3D"aal_anchor" id=
=3D"aa-the-copy-directly-from-the-artboard-method"><svg aria-hidden=3D"true=
" class=3D"aal_svg" height=3D"16" version=3D"1.1" viewBox=3D"0 0 16 16" wid=
th=3D"16"><path fill-rule=3D"evenodd" d=3D"M4 9h1v1H4c-1.5 0-3-1.69-3-3.5S2=
.55 3 4 3h4c1.45 0 3 1.69 3 3.5 0 1.41-.91 2.72-2 3.25V8.59c.58-.45 1-1.27 =
1-2.09C10 5.22 8.98 4 8 4H4c-.98 0-2 1.22-2 2.5S3 9 4 9zm9-3h-1v1h1c1 0 2 1=
.22 2 2.5S13.98 12 13 12H9c-.98 0-2-1.22-2-2.5 0-.83.42-1.64 1-2.09V6.25c-1=
.09.53-2 1.84-2 3.25C6 11.31 7.55 13 9 13h4c1.45 0 3-1.69 3-3.5S14.5 6 13 6=
z"></path></svg></a>The =E2=80=9CCopy Directly from the Artboard=E2=80=9D M=
ethod</h3>
<p>Speaking of selecting individual bits of artwork intended to be used as =
SVG, there is a slightly-lesser-known way to extract bits, and that=E2=80=
=99s as simple as <strong>Edit &gt; Copy</strong>. </p>
<figure id=3D"post-248005" class=3D"align-none media-248005"><img data-reca=
lc-dims=3D"1" decoding=3D"async" src=3D"https://i0.wp.com/css-tricks.com/wp=
-content/uploads/2016/11/paste-copied.gif?ssl=3D1" alt=3D""></figure>
<p>After copying, your clipboard will have inline SVG code on it that you c=
an paste as text. </p>
<p>The code that you get is slightly different than any method we=E2=80=99v=
e looked at so far. It=E2=80=99s closest to the =E2=80=9CSave As=E2=80=A6=
=E2=80=9D format though, in that you get the XML doctype and such. It=E2=80=
=99s not the web-optimized format we get from exporting. It does have it=E2=
=80=99s own <code>viewBox</code> cropped exactly to the elements edges. </p=
>
<p>If you use this to drop SVG right into your HTML, plan to do a little ma=
nual code cleanup.</p>
<h3><a href=3D"https://css-tricks.com/illustrator-to-svg/#aa-now-that-you-h=
ave-the-svg" aria-hidden=3D"true" class=3D"aal_anchor" id=3D"aa-now-that-yo=
u-have-the-svg"><svg aria-hidden=3D"true" class=3D"aal_svg" height=3D"16" v=
ersion=3D"1.1" viewBox=3D"0 0 16 16" width=3D"16"><path fill-rule=3D"evenod=
d" d=3D"M4 9h1v1H4c-1.5 0-3-1.69-3-3.5S2.55 3 4 3h4c1.45 0 3 1.69 3 3.5 0 1=
.41-.91 2.72-2 3.25V8.59c.58-.45 1-1.27 1-2.09C10 5.22 8.98 4 8 4H4c-.98 0-=
2 1.22-2 2.5S3 9 4 9zm9-3h-1v1h1c1 0 2 1.22 2 2.5S13.98 12 13 12H9c-.98 0-2=
-1.22-2-2.5 0-.83.42-1.64 1-2.09V6.25c-1.09.53-2 1.84-2 3.25C6 11.31 7.55 1=
3 9 13h4c1.45 0 3-1.69 3-3.5S14.5 6 13 6z"></path></svg></a>Now that you ha=
ve the SVG=E2=80=A6</h3>
<p>=E2=80=A6 now what?</p>
<ul>
<li>Perhaps you=E2=80=99re gathering individual SVG icon files for use in <=
a href=3D"https://css-tricks.com/svg-sprites-use-better-icon-fonts/">an SVG=
 icon system</a>. As in, using a build tool to process them into a single f=
ile full of <code>&lt;symbol&gt;</code>s to <code>&lt;use&gt;</code>.</li>
<li>Perhaps you needed the `.svg` to use as an <code>&lt;img&gt;</code>.</l=
i>
<li>Perhaps you needed the `.svg` to use as a <code>background-image</code>=
 in CSS.</li>
<li>Perhaps you=E2=80=99re going to drop the SVG code into HTML directly as=
 inline SVG.</li>
</ul>
<p>There are many ways to <a href=3D"https://css-tricks.com/using-svg/">use=
 SVG</a>, but that always starts with getting the SVG to work with in the f=
irst place.</p>

